Thanks for all the suggestions. There have been some good ideas.
Here are some modifications I've made based on the feedback:

Pirates can be neutral or chaotic.

I changed the name of the artifact to "The Marauder's Map" for a small
Harry Potter reference. Not that Harry Potter has anything to do with
Pirates, but I did think that the artifact does come close to the
Marauder's Map in those books.

A lot of you felt that the Map would be too powerful. But I think it's
only marginally better than a wizard with the Magic Mapping spell.

Xlvl titles:
Landlubber
Sailor
Mariner
Mutineer
Buccaneer
Cutthroat
Corsair
Privateer
Dread Pirate

I especially like the last one. Thanks K. Bailey! Maybe we could make
the Quest Leader be the Dread Pirate Roberts.

Deities: (as suggested by Andrew Fenton)
Nereus, Neptune, Proteus

I made the following tweaks to the skills:
Short sword: S -> B
Flail: U -> E
Shuriken: B -> U

New starting inventory:
+0 cutlass (scimitar)
2-3 +1 knives
+0 high boots
2 sea biscuits (cram rations)
3-6 bananas (1/4 chance of kelp fronds)
1 ring (50% chance of being adornment)
50% chance of oilskin sack, otherwise a grappling hook

Oilskin cloak, oilskin sack and grappling hook come pre-identified.

More "piratey things": (thanks to xanthian)
sack <=> ditty bag
large box <=> foot locker
club <=> belaying pin

Quest:
I like K. Bailey's idea a lot. I'll have to start working on it. Any
ideas for the nemesis's name? Are there any famous historical or
literary characters canonically opposed to pirates?

In general I need more ideas for how to distinguish pirates from other
classes. Something like the Priest ability to BUC id items, the
Archaeologist ability to note historical statues and use uncursed
touchstone, the monk ability to fast, the knight ability to jump, etc.
Or more YAFMs or anything really.

